artifact.self_contained_problem:
In high-energy electron–proton scattering, diffractive events are characterized by a colour-singlet exchange between the proton and the virtual photon, conventionally described phenomenologically by the exchange of a Regge trajectory called the pomeron. Empirically, a single "soft" pomeron with intercept α_P(0) ≈ 1.08 successfully describes hadronic total cross-sections that grow slowly with the squared centre-of-mass energy W², but it fails to describe the more rapidly rising W²-dependence of diffractive cross-sections observed when either the photon virtuality Q² or the mass of an exclusively produced vector meson becomes large.

Working within Quantum Chromodynamics (QCD) as the underlying gauge theory of the strong interaction (an SU(3) Yang–Mills theory coupled to quarks), formulate and justify a QCD-based description of the colour-singlet object exchanged in diffractive deep inelastic scattering γ*p → X p that:

(a) reproduces, in the appropriate kinematic limit, the slowly rising "soft pomeron" behaviour of total and elastic hadronic cross-sections;
(b) accounts quantitatively for the steeper rise with W² of diffractive structure functions F₂^{D(3)}(β, Q², x_P) and of exclusive vector-meson production cross-sections σ(γ*p → Vp) as Q² and/or M_V² increase;
(c) explains the apparent factorisation of the diffractive cross-section into a "pomeron flux" times a partonic structure (or specifies the precise mechanism of factorisation breaking);
(d) interpolates consistently between the non-perturbative (small Q²) and perturbative (large Q²) regimes, including a derivation of the effective intercept and slope of the pomeron trajectory from QCD dynamics (e.g. via BFKL-type resummation, two-gluon exchange, or non-perturbative gluon condensates).

Provide such a derivation, or prove that no description with the above properties exists within QCD.
